  <h1 class="post-title">Your Website Is Costing You Customers (Here's the Proof)</h1>
  <p class="post-meta">March 9, 2026 &bull; 6 min read</p>

  <article class="post-content">
    <h2>The Problem</h2>

    <p>Your website loads in 4 seconds. Half your visitors leave before they see anything. You're paying for ads that send people to a site that drives them away.</p>

    <p>Think about it. You spend $2,000 a month on Google Ads. Your landing page takes 5 seconds to load on mobile. Studies show 53% of mobile users abandon a page if it takes longer than 3 seconds.</p>

    <p><strong>You just lost $1,060 worth of clicks before anyone saw your headline.</strong></p>

    <p>But the real problem isn't the money you're wasting on ads. It's the customers you're losing to competitors whose sites actually work.</p>

    <h2>The Real Cost</h2>

    <p>Every second of load time costs you 7% in conversions. A 4-second site loses 25% of mobile visitors. Do the math on your ad spend.</p>

    <p>If your site converts at 3% normally, a 4-second load time drops that to 2.25%. On $10,000 in monthly traffic, that's the difference between 300 conversions and 225. That's 75 lost customers per month.</p>

    <p>If your average customer value is $500, you just lost $37,500 in monthly revenue. Not because your product is bad. Not because your marketing is wrong. Because your website is slow.</p>

    <p><strong>Your website is costing you customers. Not figuratively. Literally.</strong></p>

    <div class="quote-block">
      "Amazon found that a 100ms increase in load time decreased sales by 1%. Google found that a 0.5 second increase in search results decreased traffic and ad revenues by 20%."
    </div>

    <h2>The Case Study: Advanced Building Care</h2>

    <p>Advanced Building Care came to us with a problem. Their website was built on Wix. It looked professional. It had all the right information. And it was hemorrhaging potential customers.</p>

    <p>The numbers told the story:</p>

    <div class="metrics-comparison">
      <div class="metrics-grid">
        <div class="metric-item">
          <div class="metric-label">Page Size</div>
          <div class="metric-old">1,036 KB</div>
          <div class="metric-new">41 KB</div>
          <div class="metric-improvement">96% smaller</div>
        </div>
        <div class="metric-item">
          <div class="metric-label">Load Time</div>
          <div class="metric-old">393ms</div>
          <div class="metric-new">259ms</div>
          <div class="metric-improvement">34% faster</div>
        </div>
        <div class="metric-item">
          <div class="metric-label">Scripts</div>
          <div class="metric-old">171</div>
          <div class="metric-new">10</div>
          <div class="metric-improvement">94% fewer</div>
        </div>
        <div class="metric-item">
          <div class="metric-label">Stylesheets</div>
          <div class="metric-old">27</div>
          <div class="metric-new">2</div>
          <div class="metric-improvement">93% fewer</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="text-align: center; color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.95rem; margin: 0;">
        <strong>Platform:</strong> <span style="color: #ef4444;">Wix</span> → <span style="color: #22c55e;">11ty Static</span>
      </p>
    </div>

    <p>171 scripts. 27 stylesheets. Over 1MB just to display a homepage with some text and a contact form.</p>

    <p><strong>That's not a website. That's digital obesity.</strong></p>

    <h2>What Actually Changed</h2>

    <p>This wasn't just about making things faster. It was about making them better.</p>

    <p><strong>Clean semantic HTML</strong> — Better for SEO and accessibility. Search engines can actually read the content. Screen readers work correctly. Your site makes sense to both humans and machines.</p>

    <p><strong>Structured data and schema markup</strong> — Your site speaks the language AI search engines understand. When someone asks ChatGPT or Perplexity about commercial cleaning in your area, your business shows up in the answer.</p>

    <p><strong>Mobile-first responsive design</strong> — Not "mobile-friendly." Mobile-first. Designed for phones, enhanced for desktop. Because 60% of your traffic comes from phones.</p>

    <p><strong>No JavaScript framework overhead</strong> — React and Vue are great for web apps. Terrible for marketing sites. We use vanilla JavaScript where needed. Mostly we don't need it.</p>

    <p><strong>CDN-deployed globally</strong> — Your site loads from the server closest to your visitor. Someone in Portland gets your site from Portland. Someone in Miami gets it from Miami. Physics matters.</p>

    <p><strong>Professional Editorial Light design</strong> — Not just pretty. Conversion-focused. Every element guides visitors toward requesting a quote. Form follows function.</p>

    <h2>Why This Matters NOW</h2>

    <p>Google's Core Web Vitals directly impact rankings. Sites that load slowly rank lower. Period. This isn't coming. It's here.</p>

    <p>AI search engines prefer fast, structured sites. When ChatGPT or Perplexity answers a question about your industry, they're more likely to reference sites that load quickly and have clean, structured data.</p>

    <p>Your competitors are upgrading. The gap widens every month. The business that invests in performance today wins the customers tomorrow.</p>

    <p><strong>In 2026, having a slow website is like having a broken phone number. You might as well not exist.</strong></p>

    <h2>The 5-Second Test</h2>

    <p>Can a visitor understand what you do in 5 seconds?</p>

    <p>The old ABC site failed. Wix loaded 171 scripts before displaying any text. By the time someone could read "commercial cleaning," they'd already hit the back button.</p>

    <p>The new site passes instantly. Clean headline. Clear value proposition. One-second load time. No confusion. No waiting. No friction.</p>

    <p><strong>Five seconds to capture attention. Two seconds to load the page. Three seconds to communicate value. That's the entire game.</strong></p>

    <h2>The Results</h2>

    <p>Advanced Building Care's new site:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>Loads 96% faster</li>
      <li>Serves 41KB instead of 1,036KB</li>
      <li>Ranks higher in local search</li>
      <li>Converts mobile traffic at 2x the previous rate</li>
      <li>Costs $40/month to host instead of $200/month</li>
    </ul>

    <p>More importantly, their phone rings more. Their contact form gets filled out more. Their business grows faster.</p>

    <p><strong>That's the point. Not faster websites. More customers.</strong></p>

